{
  "_readme": "Plain-language -> CPV (Common Procurement Vocabulary) map. This is what generalizes the tool: any good, not one sector. Each entry lists CPV codes and the everyday words people actually use for that thing. Matching is on whole words of the user's text (a plural s or es allowed), so 'office chairs' hits 'chair' but 'healthcare' does not hit 'car'. Extend freely - add an entry, no code changes needed. CPV is the EU's official classification for public contracts; the full list has ~9,500 codes, this is a practical starter set across sectors. Optional `psc` lists US Product Service Codes (full codes, or a prefix such as \"Y1\" for a whole family) so SAM.gov rows, which carry no CPV, can match on codes too. Optional `pt` lists Portuguese terms for the same thing: Brazil's PNCP notices are in Portuguese and carry no CPV, so an English search for 'body armour' reaches them through 'colete balístico'.",
